  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to locally numerous showers and\nstorms are expected this afternoon into the early overnight\nhours and may bring a risk localized urban and small stream\nflooding as well as strong winds gusts.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft, and deep moisture characterized by PW\nvalues of 2-2.5 inches and 125-150% of normal, currently \nlocated over the western/central NC Piedmont as well as near the\nSC/GA coast will continue be directed across the Carolinas and \nsouthern Middle Atlantic ahead of a mid and upper-level trough \nthat will pivot into the OH and lower MS Valleys today and then \nweaken while drifting into the TN Valley and southern \nAppalachians through early Mon. Several MCVs will probably be \nembedded within that swly flow regime across the South Atlantic \nstates and may locally focus ascent along their track. \n\nAt the surface, moist, sly/sely flow will continue across the \nCarolinas, in a moist/warm sector downstream of a ~1003 mb low \nthat will drift across the OH Valley. A differential heating \nzone is likely to develop over the Piedmont later this afternoon\nas low overcast erodes from east to west and provide a surface \nforcing mechanism for locally numerous showers/storms.\n\nLarge scale and persistent forcing mechanisms, such as the \nelongated RRQ of the upper jet streak from the lower MS Valley \ninto the Northeast and southern Canada, will likely remain just \nwest of the forecast area amidst near neutral mid-lvl height \ntendencies. However, a deeply moist, uninhibited, and \nconditionally unstable profiles --weakly to moderately so-- will\notherwise favor the development of scattered to locally \nnumerous cells amid 25-30 kts of swly flow. A (Marginal) risk of\nwater-loaded, damaging downbursts remains possible but highly \nconditional as the thermodynamic environment will only promote \nminimal diabatic enhancement to the downdrafts and associated \nsurface wind gusts; storm organization may be required to \nproduce severe magnitude wind gusts. Although heavy downpours \nwill also result, relatively fast cell motions to the northeast \nwill counter some tendency for back-building and consequently \nlimit the overall risk of flooding. However, given the potential\nfor a favorable alignment of the differential heating boundary,\nand subsequent outflow reinforcement, to the mean-wind in the \n850-300mb layer, repeated cells training over the same area may \nbe possible to produce locally greater rainfall amounts of 2-4+ \ninches.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Showers and storms capable of producing very \nheavy rain will shift east Monday and Tuesday, with mainly \nafternoon and evening showers and storms through the remainder \nof the week.\n\nThe base of the positively tilted upper trough west of the \nAppalachians will continue to sink south toward the southern \nAppalachians on Monday, maintaining deep moist southwesterly \nflow across the region. Precipitable water will continue to \nexceed 2 inches and be as high as 2.5-2.6 inches across the \neastern half of the area. Despite weak lapse rates aloft, dew \npoints in the mid-70s will still support at least some \ninstability, with guidance suggesting 500 to perhaps 1000 J/kg \nMLCAPE by Monday afternoon. 500 mb flow also increases slightly,\nand bulk shear may be as much as 20- 25 knots. While the \nlarger-scale forcing is not particularly strong with the slow-\nmoving upper low, models suggest there may be an uptick in \nperturbations in the southwesterly flow to help kick off \nscattered to numerous showers and storms, especially across the \neast. The main threat from convection appears to be the \npotential training of storm clusters and heavy rainfall given \nthe anomalously moist air mass and nearly unidirectional \ntropospheric flow. Hi-res guidance and probability-matched mean \n(PMM) precipitation fields suggest localized areas of 1-3 inches\nwill be possible, although we have seen as much as 4-6 inches \nwith some focused cells in the foothills today.\n\nA similar pattern is likely to repeat across mainly eastern \nareas on Tuesday and to a lesser extent into Wednesday as the \nupper low lifts and weakens. Moist flow around the Bermuda high \nwill continue through the end of the week, so diurnal convection\nwill be possible each day.",
